{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019yS5str4ZWj6E3y38YySuD","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"how phone computer sync time automatically NTP\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Time synchronization for Android via TimeServer\",\"url\":\"https://www.timeservers.net/sync/ntp/android\"},{\"title\":\"Set the NTP server of your android device · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/xujiaao/63cb3bbea9fe22e79206e5eb7ba82d0e\"},{\"title\":\"ClockSync - Synchronise Device Time via NTP\",\"url\":\"https://www.xda-developers.com/clocksync-synchronise-device-time-via-ntp/\"},{\"title\":\"Time source priority | Android Open Source Project\",\"url\":\"https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/time-source\"},{\"title\":\"Sync an Android Phone’s System Clock to a Computer’s Clock Using ADB | Jorge Sintes\",\"url\":\"https://jorgesintes.xyz/posts/precisely_syncing_an_android_phones_system_clock_to_a_computers_clock_using_adb/\"},{\"title\":\"Windows Time Service Tools and Settings | Microsoft Learn\",\"url\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings\"},{\"title\":\"How to Set Your Android to Atomic Time for a Perfectly Synced Clock << Android :: Gadget Hacks\",\"url\":\"https://android.gadgethacks.com/how-to/set-your-android-atomic-time-for-perfectly-synced-clock-0170500/\"},{\"title\":\"[APP] ClockSync - synchronize device time via NTP | XDA Forums\",\"url\":\"https://xdaforums.com/t/app-clocksync-synchronize-device-time-via-ntp.688177/\"},{\"title\":\"Network time detection | Android Open Source Project\",\"url\":\"https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/time/network-time-detection\"},{\"title\":\"Why Accurate Network Time Synchronization Through NTP Is Critical\",\"url\":\"https://www.bodet-time.com/resources/blog/1757-why-accurate-network-time-synchronization-through-ntp-is-critical.html\"}]\n\n## How Phones and Computers Sync Time Automatically via NTP\n\nHere's how phones and computers automatically synchronize their time using NTP (Network Time Protocol):\n\n### How NTP Works\n\nThe user device sends a request to the synchronization server using a special network protocol called NTP (Network Time Protocol). In response to the request, the server generates an NTP packet with the required data and sends it back to the user device. The built-in synchronization feature processes the packet and adjusts the internal time of the device using the data from the NTP packet.(no content)High synchronization precision (up to one hundredth of a second, i.e., 10 milliseconds) is achieved because the time setting function takes into account the delay between sending off the request and receiving the response.\n\n### Android Devices\n\nAndroid devices automatically attempt to obtain the correct Unix epoch time from a network source. Android uses the Simple Network Time Protocol (SNTP), which uses the User Datagram Protocol (UDP), to obtain time information.(no content)Android's default network time sync uses SNTP with a single time query approximately once a day so that it always has a recent time signal.(no content)By default, AOSP uses time servers at time.android.com, which is an alias for Google Public NTP.(no content)For most Android phones, the system clock is also set using a protocol called NITZ, which relies on a connection with your carrier to ensure that the time stays in sync. The trouble is that this feature won't work when you're outside of cellular range.(no content)If the device is connected to the internet and has a SIM card, suggestions are generated using both NTP (network) and NITZ (telephony).\n\n### Windows Computers\n\nWindows Time Service (W32Time) follows the Network Time Protocol (NTP) specification, which requires the use of User Datagram Protocol (UDP) port 123 for all time synchronization.(no content)You can use Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to configure a computer to be an NTP server or NTP client, configure the time synchronization mechanism, or configure a computer to be a reliable time source.(no content)You can use the w32tm command to configure W32Time settings and diagnose computer time problems. The w32tm command is the preferred command-line tool for configuring, monitoring, and troubleshooting W32Time.\n\n### Why Time Synchronization Matters\n\nComputers often experience time drift, where the internal clock slowly drifts further away from the actual precise time. Although this might not be much of an issue if operating alone, it's when a computer communicates with another, either online or through a local network, that time drift can be a major problem.(no content)NTP time servers act as a benchmark for time on your network, distributing a time signal to synchronize all connected devices. Often, the time signal uses a standard such as UTC (Universal Coordinated Time), derived from atomic clocks or GPS service.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dbddee86-a9a5-40ab-8589-fd51456e97f2","uuid":"c6484529-ea3b-41d3-ae26-ec45c9263440","tool_use_result":{"query":"how phone computer sync time automatically NTP","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017Jo1P2g1Kspt3nyucESxdw","content":[{"title":"Time synchronization for Android via TimeServer","url":"https://www.timeservers.net/sync/ntp/android"},{"title":"Set the NTP server of your android device · GitHub","url":"https://gist.github.com/xujiaao/63cb3bbea9fe22e79206e5eb7ba82d0e"},{"title":"ClockSync - Synchronise Device Time via NTP","url":"https://www.xda-developers.com/clocksync-synchronise-device-time-via-ntp/"},{"title":"Time source priority | Android Open Source Project","url":"https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/time-source"},{"title":"Sync an Android Phone’s System Clock to a Computer’s Clock Using ADB | Jorge Sintes","url":"https://jorgesintes.xyz/posts/precisely_syncing_an_android_phones_system_clock_to_a_computers_clock_using_adb/"},{"title":"Windows Time Service Tools and Settings | Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings"},{"title":"How to Set Your Android to Atomic Time for a Perfectly Synced Clock << Android :: Gadget Hacks","url":"https://android.gadgethacks.com/how-to/set-your-android-atomic-time-for-perfectly-synced-clock-0170500/"},{"title":"[APP] ClockSync - synchronize device time via NTP | XDA Forums","url":"https://xdaforums.com/t/app-clocksync-synchronize-device-time-via-ntp.688177/"},{"title":"Network time detection | Android Open Source Project","url":"https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/time/network-time-detection"},{"title":"Why Accurate Network Time Synchronization Through NTP Is Critical","url":"https://www.bodet-time.com/resources/blog/1757-why-accurate-network-time-synchronization-through-ntp-is-critical.html"}]},"## How Phones and Computers Sync Time Automatically via NTP\n\nHere's how phones and computers automatically synchronize their time using NTP (Network Time Protocol):\n\n### How NTP Works\n\nThe user device sends a request to the synchronization server using a special network protocol called NTP (Network Time Protocol). In response to the request, the server generates an NTP packet with the required data and sends it back to the user device. The built-in synchronization feature processes the packet and adjusts the internal time of the device using the data from the NTP packet.(no content)High synchronization precision (up to one hundredth of a second, i.e., 10 milliseconds) is achieved because the time setting function takes into account the delay between sending off the request and receiving the response.\n\n### Android Devices\n\nAndroid devices automatically attempt to obtain the correct Unix epoch time from a network source. Android uses the Simple Network Time Protocol (SNTP), which uses the User Datagram Protocol (UDP), to obtain time information.(no content)Android's default network time sync uses SNTP with a single time query approximately once a day so that it always has a recent time signal.(no content)By default, AOSP uses time servers at time.android.com, which is an alias for Google Public NTP.(no content)For most Android phones, the system clock is also set using a protocol called NITZ, which relies on a connection with your carrier to ensure that the time stays in sync. The trouble is that this feature won't work when you're outside of cellular range.(no content)If the device is connected to the internet and has a SIM card, suggestions are generated using both NTP (network) and NITZ (telephony).\n\n### Windows Computers\n\nWindows Time Service (W32Time) follows the Network Time Protocol (NTP) specification, which requires the use of User Datagram Protocol (UDP) port 123 for all time synchronization.(no content)You can use Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to configure a computer to be an NTP server or NTP client, configure the time synchronization mechanism, or configure a computer to be a reliable time source.(no content)You can use the w32tm command to configure W32Time settings and diagnose computer time problems. The w32tm command is the preferred command-line tool for configuring, monitoring, and troubleshooting W32Time.\n\n### Why Time Synchronization Matters\n\nComputers often experience time drift, where the internal clock slowly drifts further away from the actual precise time. Although this might not be much of an issue if operating alone, it's when a computer communicates with another, either online or through a local network, that time drift can be a major problem.(no content)NTP time servers act as a benchmark for time on your network, distributing a time signal to synchronize all connected devices. Often, the time signal uses a standard such as UTC (Universal Coordinated Time), derived from atomic clocks or GPS service."],"durationSeconds":27.347537957999993}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RVoPBzyMeda2NR4QJCc3KT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NTP time synchronization security vulnerabilities attacks\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Network Time Protocol (NTP): Threats and countermeasures | Infosec\",\"url\":\"https://www.infosecinstitute.com/resources/general-security/network-time-protocol-ntp-threats-countermeasures/\"},{\"title\":\"NTP : Security vulnerabilities, CVEs\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvedetails.com/vulnerability-list/vendor_id-2153/NTP.html\"},{\"title\":\"Security flaws in Network Time Protocol make other (security) protocols vulnerable | Cybersecurity | SIDN\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidn.nl/en/news-and-blogs/security-flaws-in-network-time-protocol-make-other-security-protocols-vulnerable\"},{\"title\":\"Best Practices for NTP Services\",\"url\":\"https://www.sei.cmu.edu/blog/best-practices-for-ntp-services/\"},{\"title\":\"A Devil of a Time: How Vulnerable is NTP to Malicious Timeservers?\",\"url\":\"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/wp-content/uploads/ndss2021_1A-2_24302_paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NTP Vulnerabilities Reported 2023-04 [Knowledge Base]\",\"url\":\"https://kb.meinbergglobal.com/kb/time_sync/ntp/ntp_vulnerabilities_reported_2023-04\"},{\"title\":\"Page 1 of 9 Secure NTP Implementation for Power System Synchronization\",\"url\":\"https://cast.ornl.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/CAST-Technical-Bulletin-009.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"1 Preventing Time Synchronization in NTP’s Broadcast Mode\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.01783\"},{\"title\":\"Measuring the Time Source Vulnerabilities in the NTP Ecosystem | Proceedings of the 2025 ACM Internet Measurement Conference\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3730567.3764478\"},{\"title\":\"NTP Security Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.ntp.org/reflib/security/\"}]\n\nHere are the results of my search on NTP time synchronization security vulnerabilities and attacks:\n\n## NTP Security Vulnerabilities and Attacks\n\n### Main Types of Attacks\n\n**Replay Attacks and Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) Attacks**: A replay attack involves an intruder replaying one or more packets. In a man-in-the-middle attack, an intruder can intercept packets between an authentic client and server.(no content)Research has identified attacks on NTP's cryptographically-authenticated broadcast mode, including a replay attack that allows an on-path attacker to indefinitely stick a broadcast client to a specific time.(no content)**Time-Shifting Attacks**: NTP is vulnerable to so-called time shifting attacks. This has motivated proposals and standardization efforts for authenticating NTP communications and for securing NTP clients.(no content)**Spoofed NTP Responses**: Because, like DNS, NTP has traditionally relied on the stateless User Datagram Protocol (UDP), an attacker can send spoofed NTP responses to an NTP client, hoping the client will use the packet contents to set its system clock.\n\n### DDoS Amplification Attacks\n\nThe NTP DDoS amplification attack has been realized worldwide.(no content)These attacks don't rely on vulnerabilities but on poor configuration. NTP services respond to a query request for a list of monitored servers, and a single small request with a spoofed source can generate a list of 600 servers that are then sent to a target.(no content)In 2014, there were over seven million abusable NTP servers.(no content)It only takes 5,000 abusable NTP servers to generate a DDoS attack in the range of 50-400 Gbps.\n\n### Specific CVE Vulnerabilities\n\nNTP before 4.2.8p14 and 4.3.x before 4.3.100 allows remote attackers to cause a denial of service (daemon exit or system time change) by predicting transmit timestamps for use in spoofed packets. The victim must be relying on unauthenticated IPv4 time sources, and there must be an off-path attacker who can query time from the victim's ntpd instance.(no content)NTP before 4.2.8p14 allows an off-path attacker to block unauthenticated synchronization via a server mode packet with a spoofed source IP address.(no content)**Cookie Snatcher Attack**: This design vulnerability affects the on-wire protocol layer, exposing client cookie and message digest.\n\n### Malicious Timeserver Attacks\n\nEven with authentication solutions in place, NTP remains highly exposed to attacks by malicious timeservers. Attack strategies include compromising existing NTP timeservers and injecting new timeservers into the NTP timeserver pool. By gaining control over fairly few existing timeservers, an opportunistic attacker can shift time at state-level or even continent-level scale.\n\n### Countermeasures and Mitigations\n\nAll versions of ntpd server prior to 4.2.7 are vulnerable to DDoS amplification attacks by default. Upgrading to the latest version is recommended.(no content)With publication of the Network Time Security (NTS) protocol in RFC 8915, workable cryptographic security has finally been added to NTP. Effective security is badly needed to address the multiple vulnerabilities published in recent years.(no content)Network Time Security (NTS) offers server authentication and integrity verification to protect against man-in-the-middle attacks. However, NTS does not address issues related to erroneous time sources.(no content)Organizations should use the restrict directive in ntp.conf to protect the server from unauthorized or malicious activity.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dbddee86-a9a5-40ab-8589-fd51456e97f2","uuid":"1d6c1b02-c88b-478f-accd-5993488dbfd1","tool_use_result":{"query":"NTP time synchronization security vulnerabilities attacks","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MWFkpa3kVWz83n3z3Jict1","content":[{"title":"Network Time Protocol (NTP): Threats and countermeasures | Infosec","url":"https://www.infosecinstitute.com/resources/general-security/network-time-protocol-ntp-threats-countermeasures/"},{"title":"NTP : Security vulnerabilities, CVEs","url":"https://www.cvedetails.com/vulnerability-list/vendor_id-2153/NTP.html"},{"title":"Security flaws in Network Time Protocol make other (security) protocols vulnerable | Cybersecurity | SIDN","url":"https://www.sidn.nl/en/news-and-blogs/security-flaws-in-network-time-protocol-make-other-security-protocols-vulnerable"},{"title":"Best Practices for NTP Services","url":"https://www.sei.cmu.edu/blog/best-practices-for-ntp-services/"},{"title":"A Devil of a Time: How Vulnerable is NTP to Malicious Timeservers?","url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/wp-content/uploads/ndss2021_1A-2_24302_paper.pdf"},{"title":"NTP Vulnerabilities Reported 2023-04 [Knowledge Base]","url":"https://kb.meinbergglobal.com/kb/time_sync/ntp/ntp_vulnerabilities_reported_2023-04"},{"title":"Page 1 of 9 Secure NTP Implementation for Power System Synchronization","url":"https://cast.ornl.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/CAST-Technical-Bulletin-009.pdf"},{"title":"1 Preventing Time Synchronization in NTP’s Broadcast Mode","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.01783"},{"title":"Measuring the Time Source Vulnerabilities in the NTP Ecosystem | Proceedings of the 2025 ACM Internet Measurement Conference","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3730567.3764478"},{"title":"NTP Security Analysis","url":"https://www.ntp.org/reflib/security/"}]},"Here are the results of my search on NTP time synchronization security vulnerabilities and attacks:\n\n## NTP Security Vulnerabilities and Attacks\n\n### Main Types of Attacks\n\n**Replay Attacks and Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) Attacks**: A replay attack involves an intruder replaying one or more packets. In a man-in-the-middle attack, an intruder can intercept packets between an authentic client and server.(no content)Research has identified attacks on NTP's cryptographically-authenticated broadcast mode, including a replay attack that allows an on-path attacker to indefinitely stick a broadcast client to a specific time.(no content)**Time-Shifting Attacks**: NTP is vulnerable to so-called time shifting attacks. This has motivated proposals and standardization efforts for authenticating NTP communications and for securing NTP clients.(no content)**Spoofed NTP Responses**: Because, like DNS, NTP has traditionally relied on the stateless User Datagram Protocol (UDP), an attacker can send spoofed NTP responses to an NTP client, hoping the client will use the packet contents to set its system clock.\n\n### DDoS Amplification Attacks\n\nThe NTP DDoS amplification attack has been realized worldwide.(no content)These attacks don't rely on vulnerabilities but on poor configuration. NTP services respond to a query request for a list of monitored servers, and a single small request with a spoofed source can generate a list of 600 servers that are then sent to a target.(no content)In 2014, there were over seven million abusable NTP servers.(no content)It only takes 5,000 abusable NTP servers to generate a DDoS attack in the range of 50-400 Gbps.\n\n### Specific CVE Vulnerabilities\n\nNTP before 4.2.8p14 and 4.3.x before 4.3.100 allows remote attackers to cause a denial of service (daemon exit or system time change) by predicting transmit timestamps for use in spoofed packets. The victim must be relying on unauthenticated IPv4 time sources, and there must be an off-path attacker who can query time from the victim's ntpd instance.(no content)NTP before 4.2.8p14 allows an off-path attacker to block unauthenticated synchronization via a server mode packet with a spoofed source IP address.(no content)**Cookie Snatcher Attack**: This design vulnerability affects the on-wire protocol layer, exposing client cookie and message digest.\n\n### Malicious Timeserver Attacks\n\nEven with authentication solutions in place, NTP remains highly exposed to attacks by malicious timeservers. Attack strategies include compromising existing NTP timeservers and injecting new timeservers into the NTP timeserver pool. By gaining control over fairly few existing timeservers, an opportunistic attacker can shift time at state-level or even continent-level scale.\n\n### Countermeasures and Mitigations\n\nAll versions of ntpd server prior to 4.2.7 are vulnerable to DDoS amplification attacks by default. Upgrading to the latest version is recommended.(no content)With publication of the Network Time Security (NTS) protocol in RFC 8915, workable cryptographic security has finally been added to NTP. Effective security is badly needed to address the multiple vulnerabilities published in recent years.(no content)Network Time Security (NTS) offers server authentication and integrity verification to protect against man-in-the-middle attacks. However, NTS does not address issues related to erroneous time sources.(no content)Organizations should use the restrict directive in ntp.conf to protect the server from unauthorized or malicious activity."],"durationSeconds":33.909262082999994}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuctWpCvW8YHBWVQCxpkkd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NTP Network Time Protocol how it works explained 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Network Time Protocol - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Time_Protocol\"},{\"title\":\"How NTP Works\",\"url\":\"https://www.ntp.org/documentation/4.2.8-series/warp/\"},{\"title\":\"Network Time Protocol (NTP) - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/computer-networks/network-time-protocol-ntp/\"},{\"title\":\"What is Network Time Protocol (NTP)?\",\"url\":\"https://www.techtarget.com/searchnetworking/definition/Network-Time-Protocol\"},{\"title\":\"Network Time Protocol (NTP) | NetworkAcademy.IO\",\"url\":\"https://www.networkacademy.io/ccna/network-services/network-time-protocol-ntp\"},{\"title\":\"What is NTP (Network Time Protocol)?\",\"url\":\"https://www.pubnub.com/learn/glossary/ntp-protocol/\"},{\"title\":\"How Does NTP Work? | Kevin Sookocheff\",\"url\":\"https://sookocheff.com/post/time/how-does-ntp-work/\"},{\"title\":\"Network Time Protocol (NTP)\",\"url\":\"https://www.meinbergglobal.com/english/info/ntp.htm\"},{\"title\":\"What Is NTP? How Does It Synchronize Time? - Huawei\",\"url\":\"https://info.support.huawei.com/info-finder/encyclopedia/en/NTP.html\"},{\"title\":\"What Is NTP? A Beginner's Guide to Network Time Protocol\",\"url\":\"https://www.galsys.co.uk/news/what-is-ntp-a-beginners-guide-to-network-time-protocol/\"}]\n\n# Network Time Protocol (NTP) - How It Works\n\n## What is NTP?\n\nThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) is a networking protocol for clock synchronization between computer systems over packet-switched, variable-latency data networks.(no content)In operation since before 1985, NTP is one of the oldest Internet protocols in current use. NTP was designed by David L. Mills of the University of Delaware.(no content)NTP is intended to synchronize participating computers to within a few milliseconds of Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). It uses the intersection algorithm, a modified version of Marzullo's algorithm, to select accurate time servers and is designed to mitigate the effects of variable network latency. NTP can usually maintain time to within tens of milliseconds over the public Internet, and can achieve better than one millisecond accuracy in local area networks under ideal conditions.\n\n## How NTP Works\n\n### Hierarchical Stratum System\n\nNTP uses a hierarchical network architecture that forms a tree structure. Each level of this hierarchy is called a stratum and is assigned a number starting with zero representing reference hardware clocks.(no content)At the topmost level, there are highly accurate time resources such as atomic or GPS clocks. These clock resources are called stratum 0 servers, and they are linked to the below NTP servers called Stratum 1, 2, or 3 and so on. These servers then provide the accurate date and time so that communicating hosts are synced to each other.(no content)The stratum is essential because it indicates the distance from the reference clock and helps determine the network's accuracy, reliability, and hierarchy of time sources. The lower the stratum number, the more accurate the time source. Each time a device synchronizes with a server, small delays and inaccuracies are introduced, so higher-stratum devices have slightly less precise time than lower-stratum ones.\n\n### The Time Synchronization Process\n\nNTP uses IP/UDP on destination port 123 to exchange NTP timestamps and accurately calculate the current time within microseconds.(no content)The peer process runs the on-wire protocol that uses four raw timestamps: the origin timestamp T1 upon departure of the client request, the receive timestamp T2 upon arrival at the server, the transmit timestamp T3 upon departure of the server reply, and the destination timestamp T4 upon arrival at the client.(no content)The client sends an NTP request packet to the time server, stamping the time as it does so (the origin timestamp). The server stamps the time when the request packet is received (the receive timestamp). The server stamps the time again when it sends a response packet back to the client (the transmit timestamp). The client stamps the time when the response packet is received (the destination timestamp). This process may only take microseconds, but the timestamps allow the client to account for the roundtrip delay and work out the difference between its internal time and that provided by the server.\n\n### Clock Discipline Algorithm\n\nAn NTP client receives time data from one or more connected servers and uses that time data to compute a phase or frequency correction to apply to the local clock. If the time correction is only a slight change, the correction is applied gradually (called slewing) in order to avoid clock jumps. If the local clock is off by a large amount, the adjustment may be applied all at once. The adjustment to the local clock is called the clock discipline algorithm.(no content)NTP works best when a device's clock is already close to the accurate time: The protocol is designed to adjust the time gradually in tiny steps, minimizing abrupt changes that could disrupt processes dependent on precise timing.\n\n## Current Version\n\nNTPv4 is the current version of NTP. The Internet Engineering Task Force introduced NTPv4 in Request for Comments (RFC) 5905 in 2010. It is backward-compatible with the previous version, NTPv3.(no content)NTPv4 has a modified protocol header that supports the IP version 6 address family. This latest version improved mitigation and discipline algorithms that enhance accuracy to tens of microseconds. NTPv4 also has a server discovery function that simplifies identifying the configuration of a server.\n\n## SNTP (Simple Network Time Protocol)\n\nNTP may be too complex for some systems. For them, Simple Network Time Protocol (SNTP) is available. It is NTP minus a few internal components. SNTP synchronizes to NTP servers. It was developed for smaller, less powerful computers and needs less memory and CPU resources than NTP.\n\n## Common Uses\n\nNTP ensures that the time on all participating computers is accurate and synchronized. This is critical for various applications that rely on precise timekeeping, such as logging events, securing communications, and coordinating distributed systems.(no content)NTP time synchronization services are widely available in the public Internet. The public NTP subnet currently includes several thousand servers in most countries and on every continent of the globe, including Antarctica. These servers support, directly or indirectly, a total population estimated at over 25 million computers in the global Internet.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dbddee86-a9a5-40ab-8589-fd51456e97f2","uuid":"fc01ec74-ce48-464e-b26e-fd00f58e6b2f","tool_use_result":{"query":"NTP Network Time Protocol how it works explained 2024 2025","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01SjqB1Fuj72ZC11DXQR1CEz","content":[{"title":"Network Time Protocol - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Time_Protocol"},{"title":"How NTP Works","url":"https://www.ntp.org/documentation/4.2.8-series/warp/"},{"title":"Network Time Protocol (NTP) - GeeksforGeeks","url":"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/computer-networks/network-time-protocol-ntp/"},{"title":"What is Network Time Protocol (NTP)?","url":"https://www.techtarget.com/searchnetworking/definition/Network-Time-Protocol"},{"title":"Network Time Protocol (NTP) | NetworkAcademy.IO","url":"https://www.networkacademy.io/ccna/network-services/network-time-protocol-ntp"},{"title":"What is NTP (Network Time Protocol)?","url":"https://www.pubnub.com/learn/glossary/ntp-protocol/"},{"title":"How Does NTP Work? | Kevin Sookocheff","url":"https://sookocheff.com/post/time/how-does-ntp-work/"},{"title":"Network Time Protocol (NTP)","url":"https://www.meinbergglobal.com/english/info/ntp.htm"},{"title":"What Is NTP? How Does It Synchronize Time? - Huawei","url":"https://info.support.huawei.com/info-finder/encyclopedia/en/NTP.html"},{"title":"What Is NTP? A Beginner's Guide to Network Time Protocol","url":"https://www.galsys.co.uk/news/what-is-ntp-a-beginners-guide-to-network-time-protocol/"}]},"# Network Time Protocol (NTP) - How It Works\n\n## What is NTP?\n\nThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) is a networking protocol for clock synchronization between computer systems over packet-switched, variable-latency data networks.(no content)In operation since before 1985, NTP is one of the oldest Internet protocols in current use. NTP was designed by David L. Mills of the University of Delaware.(no content)NTP is intended to synchronize participating computers to within a few milliseconds of Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). It uses the intersection algorithm, a modified version of Marzullo's algorithm, to select accurate time servers and is designed to mitigate the effects of variable network latency. NTP can usually maintain time to within tens of milliseconds over the public Internet, and can achieve better than one millisecond accuracy in local area networks under ideal conditions.\n\n## How NTP Works\n\n### Hierarchical Stratum System\n\nNTP uses a hierarchical network architecture that forms a tree structure. Each level of this hierarchy is called a stratum and is assigned a number starting with zero representing reference hardware clocks.(no content)At the topmost level, there are highly accurate time resources such as atomic or GPS clocks. These clock resources are called stratum 0 servers, and they are linked to the below NTP servers called Stratum 1, 2, or 3 and so on. These servers then provide the accurate date and time so that communicating hosts are synced to each other.(no content)The stratum is essential because it indicates the distance from the reference clock and helps determine the network's accuracy, reliability, and hierarchy of time sources. The lower the stratum number, the more accurate the time source. Each time a device synchronizes with a server, small delays and inaccuracies are introduced, so higher-stratum devices have slightly less precise time than lower-stratum ones.\n\n### The Time Synchronization Process\n\nNTP uses IP/UDP on destination port 123 to exchange NTP timestamps and accurately calculate the current time within microseconds.(no content)The peer process runs the on-wire protocol that uses four raw timestamps: the origin timestamp T1 upon departure of the client request, the receive timestamp T2 upon arrival at the server, the transmit timestamp T3 upon departure of the server reply, and the destination timestamp T4 upon arrival at the client.(no content)The client sends an NTP request packet to the time server, stamping the time as it does so (the origin timestamp). The server stamps the time when the request packet is received (the receive timestamp). The server stamps the time again when it sends a response packet back to the client (the transmit timestamp). The client stamps the time when the response packet is received (the destination timestamp). This process may only take microseconds, but the timestamps allow the client to account for the roundtrip delay and work out the difference between its internal time and that provided by the server.\n\n### Clock Discipline Algorithm\n\nAn NTP client receives time data from one or more connected servers and uses that time data to compute a phase or frequency correction to apply to the local clock. If the time correction is only a slight change, the correction is applied gradually (called slewing) in order to avoid clock jumps. If the local clock is off by a large amount, the adjustment may be applied all at once. The adjustment to the local clock is called the clock discipline algorithm.(no content)NTP works best when a device's clock is already close to the accurate time: The protocol is designed to adjust the time gradually in tiny steps, minimizing abrupt changes that could disrupt processes dependent on precise timing.\n\n## Current Version\n\nNTPv4 is the current version of NTP. The Internet Engineering Task Force introduced NTPv4 in Request for Comments (RFC) 5905 in 2010. It is backward-compatible with the previous version, NTPv3.(no content)NTPv4 has a modified protocol header that supports the IP version 6 address family. This latest version improved mitigation and discipline algorithms that enhance accuracy to tens of microseconds. NTPv4 also has a server discovery function that simplifies identifying the configuration of a server.\n\n## SNTP (Simple Network Time Protocol)\n\nNTP may be too complex for some systems. For them, Simple Network Time Protocol (SNTP) is available. It is NTP minus a few internal components. SNTP synchronizes to NTP servers. It was developed for smaller, less powerful computers and needs less memory and CPU resources than NTP.\n\n## Common Uses\n\nNTP ensures that the time on all participating computers is accurate and synchronized. This is critical for various applications that rely on precise timekeeping, such as logging events, securing communications, and coordinating distributed systems.(no content)NTP time synchronization services are widely available in the public Internet. The public NTP subnet currently includes several thousand servers in most countries and on every continent of the globe, including Antarctica. These servers support, directly or indirectly, a total population estimated at over 25 million computers in the global Internet."],"durationSeconds":38.341779667000004}}
